# 💬 Real-Time Chat Feature — Implementation Plan

## Overview

Add a real-time chat system that allows the **SOS alert sender** (guest/user) to communicate directly with the **Admin** during an active emergency. Messages are stored in Firestore and synced in real-time using `onSnapshot`.

---

## 🗄️ Database Design (Firestore)

### Option A — Subcollection per Alert ✅ (Recommended)

```
alerts/{alertId}/messages/{messageId}
```

Each message document:

| Field        | Type        | Description                                    |
|------------- |------------ |----------------------------------------------- |
| `text`       | `string`    | The message content                            |
| `sender`     | `string`    | `"user"` or `"admin"`                          |
| `senderName` | `string`    | Display name (e.g. "Room 302 Guest", "Admin")  |
| `timestamp`  | `Timestamp` | `serverTimestamp()` — used for ordering        |

> [!TIP]
> **Why subcollections?** Each alert gets its own chat thread. No filtering needed — just subscribe to `alerts/{id}/messages` sorted by `timestamp`. This is the most scalable and cleanest Firestore pattern for 1:1 chat.

### Why NOT a flat `messages` collection?

A flat collection would require a composite index on `alertId + timestamp` and would mix all chats together. Subcollections are naturally scoped, cheaper to query, and match Firestore best practices.

---

## 📐 Architecture

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User as SOS User (index.html)
    participant Firestore as Firestore DB
    participant Admin as Admin (admin.html)

    User->>Firestore: addDoc(alerts/{id}/messages, {text, sender: "user"})
    Firestore-->>Admin: onSnapshot(alerts/{id}/messages) → new message
    Admin->>Firestore: addDoc(alerts/{id}/messages, {text, sender: "admin"})
    Firestore-->>User: onSnapshot(alerts/{id}/messages) → new message
```

